BT /F1 10.15 Tf 0.08 0.15 0.24 rg 1 0 0 1 50 679.0 Tm (Linux initially used the Minix filesystem, whose filename and size constraints became) Tj ET
BT /F1 10.15 Tf 0.08 0.15 0.24 rg 1 0 0 1 50 662.0 Tm (restrictive. The first extended filesystem followed, and the Second Extended Filesystem was) Tj ET
BT /F1 10.15 Tf 0.08 0.15 0.24 rg 1 0 0 1 50 645.0 Tm (released in January 1993. Linux documentation attributes the rewrite to Remy Card, Theodore) Tj ET
BT /F1 10.15 Tf 0.08 0.15 0.24 rg 1 0 0 1 50 628.0 Tm (Ts'o and Stephen Tweedie. Ext2 supplied a more Unix-like filesystem suited to the expanding) Tj ET
BT /F1 10.15 Tf 0.08 0.15 0.24 rg 1 0 0 1 50 611.0 Tm (use of Linux on larger disks, with better allocation policy and metadata capabilities.) Tj ET
BT /F1 10.15 Tf 0.08 0.15 0.24 rg 1 0 0 1 50 582.0 Tm (Ext2 became the dominant Linux disk format during the 1990s. Its design supported later) Tj ET
BT /F1 10.15 Tf 0.08 0.15 0.24 rg 1 0 0 1 50 565.0 Tm (compatible evolution: ext3 added journalling, and ext4 added extents and modern feature) Tj ET
BT /F1 10.15 Tf 0.08 0.15 0.24 rg 1 0 0 1 50 548.0 Tm (flags. Ext2 without journalling remained useful for certain flash media, boot areas and) Tj ET
BT /F1 10.15 Tf 0.08 0.15 0.24 rg 1 0 0 1 50 531.0 Tm (constrained devices, but it does not inherit the safety properties of ext3 or ext4 just) Tj ET
BT /F1 10.15 Tf 0.08 0.15 0.24 rg 1 0 0 1 50 514.0 Tm (because their format ancestry is shared.) Tj ET
